Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:08.46,Title,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,445)\fad(600,500)\bord3\blur0.5}ODYSSEY — BOOK XX
Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:08.46,Deck,,0,0,0,,{\pos(960,565)\fad(600,500)}The night before;\Nthe omens of the last day; the ox-hoof; Theoclymenus sees the dark.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.46,0:21:11.23,Header,,0,0,0,,{\fad(800,500)}HOMER’S ODYSSEY  ·  BOOK XX
Dialogue: 1,0:00:08.46,0:00:11.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But noble Odysseus made his bed in the fore-hall:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:11.90,0:00:18.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he spread an undressed oxhide down, and over it many\Nfleeces of the sheep the Achaeans kept slaughtering,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:18.90,0:00:22.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and Eurynome threw a mantle over him where he lay.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:22.10,0:00:28.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There Odysseus lay awake, his heart devising evils\Nfor the suitors; and out of the hall came the women,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:28.88,0:00:32.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the ones who all along had been sleeping with the suitors,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:32.26,0:00:35.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}setting each other laughing, trading their good cheer.
Dialogue: 1,0:00:35.62,0:00:40.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the heart was roused in his breast,\Nand he weighed it long in his mind and in his heart —
Dialogue: 1,0:00:40.96,0:00:46.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}whether to spring after them and deal out death to each,\Nor let them mix with the overweening suitors
Dialogue: 1,0:00:46.36,0:00:53.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}one final and last time. And the heart inside him snarled —\Nas a bitch standing over her soft-boned pups,
Dialogue: 1,0:00:53.44,0:00:57.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}facing a man she does not know,\Nsnarls and is wild to fight:
Dialogue: 1,0:00:57.36,0:01:01.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}so his heart snarled within him,\Nin outrage at their evil doings.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:01.78,0:01:04.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But he struck his chest and called his heart to order:
Dialogue: 1,0:01:04.76,0:01:08.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Bear up, my heart.\NA more dog-vile thing you bore than this
Dialogue: 1,0:01:08.66,0:01:14.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the day the Cyclops, his force past holding,\Nate my brave companions — and you endured it, till cunning
Dialogue: 1,0:01:14.38,0:01:17.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}led you out of the cave where you thought to die."
Dialogue: 1,0:01:17.28,0:01:23.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke, reproving the dear heart in his breast;\Nand his heart, in deep obedience, held fast
Dialogue: 1,0:01:23.22,0:01:28.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and endured without a break. But the\Nman himself turned this way and that.
Dialogue: 1,0:01:28.14,0:01:33.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As when a man before a great blazing fire\Nturns a paunch packed full of fat and blood
Dialogue: 1,0:01:33.00,0:01:38.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}this way and that, longing to have it roasted quickly,\Nso he rolled from side to side, turning over
Dialogue: 1,0:01:38.82,0:01:45.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}how he could lay his hands on the shameless suitors,\None man against so many. And Athena came close to him,
Dialogue: 1,0:01:45.46,0:01:51.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}down from the sky; and in body she was like a woman;\Nand she stood above his head and spoke to him:
Dialogue: 1,0:01:51.00,0:01:53.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Why awake again, most luckless of all men?
Dialogue: 1,0:01:53.72,0:01:59.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Here is your house; here in the house is your wife,\Nand your child — a son such as any man would pray for."
Dialogue: 1,0:01:59.88,0:02:02.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:02.58,0:02:05.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Yes, goddess — all this you have said as it should be.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:05.80,0:02:08.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But there is a thing my heart keeps turning over:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:08.28,0:02:14.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}how I am to lay hands on the shameless suitors — I,\None man alone; and they always inside in a crowd.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:14.54,0:02:17.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And this greater thing besides I keep turning over:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:17.38,0:02:20.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}even if I kill them, by the will of Zeus and by yours,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:20.80,0:02:24.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}where do I run from what follows?\NThink that out for me, I say."
Dialogue: 1,0:02:24.76,0:02:27.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:27.60,0:02:33.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Hard man! A person will trust a weaker friend than me,\None who is mortal, and does not know such counsels;
Dialogue: 1,0:02:33.76,0:02:38.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but I am a god, and I guard you through to the\Nend of every labor. I will tell you outright:
Dialogue: 1,0:02:38.78,0:02:43.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}if fifty companies of mortal men stood round\Nthe two of us, hot to kill us in the fight,
Dialogue: 1,0:02:43.64,0:02:47.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}even so you would drive away their cattle and fat sheep.
Dialogue: 1,0:02:47.44,0:02:50.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Come — let sleep take you too. There is misery in watching
Dialogue: 1,0:02:50.40,0:02:53.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}all night awake. You will rise up out of your evils now."
Dialogue: 1,0:02:53.60,0:03:00.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So she spoke, and poured down sleep upon his eyelids,\Nand went back herself to Olympus, the shining goddess.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:00.28,0:03:07.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And as sleep seized him, loosing the cares of his heart,\Nloosing his limbs, his true-hearted wife awoke,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:07.12,0:03:09.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and wept, sitting up on her soft bed.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:09.84,0:03:15.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And when she had had her fill of weeping,\Nfirst to Artemis prayed that woman shining among women:
Dialogue: 1,0:03:15.90,0:03:19.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Artemis, sovereign goddess,\Ndaughter of Zeus — would that now
Dialogue: 1,0:03:19.44,0:03:25.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}you would plant an arrow in my breast and take my\Nlife this very instant — or a storm-wind snatch me up
Dialogue: 1,0:03:25.56,0:03:31.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and carry me off down the ways of gloom and fling\Nme down where backward-flowing Ocean empties.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:31.34,0:03:34.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As when the storm-winds carried off Pandareus' daughters:
Dialogue: 1,0:03:34.56,0:03:37.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the gods had destroyed their parents, and they were left
Dialogue: 1,0:03:37.22,0:03:43.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}orphans in the halls; and shining Aphrodite reared\Nthem on cheese and sweet honey and pleasant wine,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:43.22,0:03:48.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and Hera gave them beauty and good sense beyond\Nall women, and pure Artemis their height,
Dialogue: 1,0:03:48.36,0:03:50.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and Athena taught them the making of glorious work.
Dialogue: 1,0:03:50.94,0:03:54.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But while shining Aphrodite was on her way to high Olympus
Dialogue: 1,0:03:54.12,0:03:58.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to ask for the girls the crown of blossoming marriage —
Dialogue: 1,0:03:58.06,0:04:02.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}gone to Zeus who delights in thunder,\Nfor he knows all things well,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:02.20,0:04:08.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the fortune and the misfortune of mortal men —\Nmeanwhile the snatching-winds carried the girls away
Dialogue: 1,0:04:08.06,0:04:11.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and gave them over to the hateful Furies, to serve them.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:11.86,0:04:18.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So may the dwellers on Olympus blot me out of sight,\Nor fair-braided Artemis strike me, that with Odysseus
Dialogue: 1,0:04:18.06,0:04:24.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}before my eyes I may pass even under the hateful earth,\Nand never gladden the mind of a lesser man.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:24.74,0:04:30.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And yet an evil is bearable, when a person weeps through\Nthe days, grieved to the packed core of the heart,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:30.64,0:04:33.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the nights hold sleep at least —\Nfor sleep makes one forget
Dialogue: 1,0:04:33.88,0:04:38.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}everything, the good and the bad,\Nonce it has wrapped the eyelids —
Dialogue: 1,0:04:38.38,0:04:41.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but to me the god sends evil even in dreams.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:41.86,0:04:45.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For this very night one slept beside me — like him,
Dialogue: 1,0:04:45.40,0:04:48.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}his very likeness as he was when\Nhe went with the army; and my heart
Dialogue: 1,0:04:48.76,0:04:53.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}rejoiced: I thought it no dream,\Nbut the waking truth at last."
Dialogue: 1,0:04:53.18,0:04:57.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So she spoke; and at once Dawn\Nof the golden throne came on.
Dialogue: 1,0:04:57.28,0:05:00.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And noble Odysseus caught the sound of her weeping,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:00.12,0:05:03.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and mused; and it seemed to him,\Nin the drift of his heart,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:03.26,0:05:06.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that she knew him already, and stood beside his head.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:06.98,0:05:12.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He gathered up the mantle and the fleeces he had slept\Nin and set them on a chair in the hall; the oxhide
Dialogue: 1,0:05:12.70,0:05:17.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he carried outside and laid down;\Nand lifting his hands he prayed to Zeus:
Dialogue: 1,0:05:17.42,0:05:20.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Father Zeus — if you gods willed it, and brought me home
Dialogue: 1,0:05:20.44,0:05:26.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}over dry land and water, after all your hard usage,\Nlet someone of the people waking in the house speak me
Dialogue: 1,0:05:26.42,0:05:30.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a word of omen; and outside,\Nlet another sign of Zeus appear."
Dialogue: 1,0:05:30.86,0:05:36.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he prayed; and Zeus the counselor heard him,\Nand at once he thundered out of gleaming Olympus,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:36.60,0:05:41.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}down from on high, out of the clouds.\NAnd noble Odysseus was glad.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:41.08,0:05:44.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the word of omen came from\Na woman grinding at the mill,
Dialogue: 1,0:05:44.26,0:05:47.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}close by, where the mills of the\Nshepherd of the people stood.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:47.48,0:05:53.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Twelve women in all kept them turning,\Nmaking barley meal and wheat flour, the marrow of men.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:53.26,0:05:58.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}The others were asleep, their wheat all ground;\Nonly one had not finished — the frailest one of all.
Dialogue: 1,0:05:58.94,0:06:02.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}She stopped her stone now,\Nand spoke the word — a sign for her master:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:02.80,0:06:05.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Father Zeus, you who rule the gods and mankind:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:05.74,0:06:11.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}how loud you thundered out of the starry sky —\Nand no cloud anywhere! You show this as a sign to someone.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:11.98,0:06:15.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then accomplish now for wretched me the word I say:
Dialogue: 1,0:06:15.30,0:06:19.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}may the suitors, on this day,\Nfor the last and latest time,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:19.02,0:06:21.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}take up their lovely feasting in the halls of Odysseus —
Dialogue: 1,0:06:21.92,0:06:24.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the men who have loosed my knees with heart-grinding labor
Dialogue: 1,0:06:24.98,0:06:28.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as I make their barley meal: may they sup now their last!"
Dialogue: 1,0:06:28.76,0:06:32.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So she spoke; and noble Odysseus\Nrejoiced at the omen of her word
Dialogue: 1,0:06:32.90,0:06:36.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and at the thunder of Zeus.\NHe told himself the guilty would pay.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:36.88,0:06:40.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Now the other slave-women, gathering\Nin the beautiful house of Odysseus,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:40.76,0:06:44.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}were kindling on the hearth the never-wearying fire.
Dialogue: 1,0:06:44.76,0:06:48.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Telemachus rose from his bed, a man like a god,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:48.20,0:06:51.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and put on his clothes, and hung\Nhis sharp sword at his shoulder,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:51.60,0:06:55.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and under his shining feet he bound the beautiful sandals,
Dialogue: 1,0:06:55.12,0:07:01.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and took his strong spear, tipped with sharpened bronze,\Nand paused on the threshold, and said to Eurycleia:
Dialogue: 1,0:07:01.84,0:07:04.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Dear nurse, have you honored the stranger in our house
Dialogue: 1,0:07:04.54,0:07:08.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}with bed and food? Or does he lie untended, as he may?
Dialogue: 1,0:07:08.48,0:07:11.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}That is my mother's way — sensible woman though she is:
Dialogue: 1,0:07:11.58,0:07:17.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}she honors the worse of mortal men on impulse,\Nand sends the better man away unhonored."
Dialogue: 1,0:07:17.70,0:07:20.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then watchful-minded Eurycleia answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:07:20.26,0:07:23.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Child, do not blame the blameless woman now.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:23.08,0:07:26.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He sat and drank his wine as long as he himself would;
Dialogue: 1,0:07:26.36,0:07:29.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and bread — he said he hungered for none: she asked him.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:29.86,0:07:34.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And when he began to think of rest and sleep,\Nshe told the slave-women to spread him a proper bed;
Dialogue: 1,0:07:34.86,0:07:41.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but he — like a man wholly wretched, out of luck —\Nrefused to sleep on a bedstead under blankets:
Dialogue: 1,0:07:41.14,0:07:43.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}on an undressed oxhide and the fleeces of sheep
Dialogue: 1,0:07:43.60,0:07:47.78,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he slept in the fore-hall.\NWe laid a mantle over him ourselves."
Dialogue: 1,0:07:47.78,0:07:54.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So she spoke; and Telemachus went out through the hall,\Nspear in hand; two swift dogs followed with him,
Dialogue: 1,0:07:54.34,0:07:58.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and he went to the assembly-place,\Nto the strong-greaved Achaeans.
Dialogue: 1,0:07:58.26,0:08:02.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And she called out to the slave-women,\Nthat woman shining among women —
Dialogue: 1,0:08:02.46,0:08:05.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Eurycleia, daughter of Ops, Peisenor's son:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:05.12,0:08:11.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Come — to work! Some of you sweep the house, and briskly,\Nand sprinkle it, and lay the crimson coverings
Dialogue: 1,0:08:11.80,0:08:18.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}on the well-made chairs; others take sponges and\Nwipe down all the tables, and rinse the mixing-bowls
Dialogue: 1,0:08:18.18,0:08:21.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the wrought double-handled cups;\Nand the rest of you away
Dialogue: 1,0:08:21.28,0:08:25.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for water at the spring —\Ngo quick, and quick back with it.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:25.18,0:08:27.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}The suitors will not be long out of the hall this morning:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:27.82,0:08:31.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they will come very early.\NIt is festival for everyone today."
Dialogue: 1,0:08:31.68,0:08:34.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So she spoke; and they listened well and obeyed:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:34.82,0:08:40.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}twenty went off to the spring of dark water,\Nand the others worked away skillfully about the house.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:40.14,0:08:45.88,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the Achaeans' serving-men came in, and split firewood,\Nwell and skillfully; and the women came back
Dialogue: 1,0:08:45.88,0:08:52.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}from the spring; and after them came the swineherd,\Ndriving three fatted hogs, the best of all he had.
Dialogue: 1,0:08:52.26,0:08:55.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He left them to feed at large in the fine yard-fences,
Dialogue: 1,0:08:55.32,0:08:58.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and himself spoke to Odysseus, and his words were kind:
Dialogue: 1,0:08:58.80,0:09:01.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Stranger — do the Achaeans look on you any better now,
Dialogue: 1,0:09:01.94,0:09:05.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}or do they still dishonor you\Nthrough the hall, as before?"
Dialogue: 1,0:09:05.54,0:09:08.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:09:08.34,0:09:13.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Ah, Eumaeus, may the gods pay back the outrage\Nthese men in their insolence keep devising —
Dialogue: 1,0:09:13.52,0:09:17.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}reckless things, in another man's house.\NThey have no share of shame."
Dialogue: 1,0:09:17.82,0:09:20.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So they spoke to one another of such things.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:20.12,0:09:25.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Melanthius came up close to them, the goatherd,\Ndriving the goats that stood out in all the herds,
Dialogue: 1,0:09:25.86,0:09:29.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a dinner for the suitors;\Nand two herdsmen followed with him.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:29.18,0:09:35.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He tethered the goats under the echoing porch,\Nand himself spoke to Odysseus, and his words were taunts:
Dialogue: 1,0:09:35.54,0:09:41.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Stranger — still here? Still going to plague this house,\Nbegging from the men? Still not out the door?
Dialogue: 1,0:09:41.16,0:09:43.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I don't imagine, now, we two will settle this business
Dialogue: 1,0:09:43.74,0:09:46.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}before we've tasted each other's\Nfists. There is no decency
Dialogue: 1,0:09:46.86,0:09:50.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in the way you beg. And there are\Nother feasts among the Achaeans."
Dialogue: 1,0:09:50.70,0:09:54.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and Odysseus of\Nthe many designs said nothing:
Dialogue: 1,0:09:54.68,0:09:58.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he only shook his head, silent,\Nbrooding evils deep in his heart.
Dialogue: 1,0:09:58.72,0:10:02.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And a third came in upon them —\NPhiloetius, a leader of men,
Dialogue: 1,0:10:02.94,0:10:06.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}driving a barren heifer for the suitors, and fat goats.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:06.34,0:10:11.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Ferrymen had brought them over — the ones who\Ncarry other travelers too, whoever comes to them.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:11.60,0:10:17.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He tethered his beasts well under the echoing porch,\Nand came up close to the swineherd and asked him:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:17.16,0:10:22.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Swineherd — who is this stranger, newly come to\Nour house? What men does he claim for his people?
Dialogue: 1,0:10:22.56,0:10:26.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Where are his family and his fathers' fields?
Dialogue: 1,0:10:26.00,0:10:30.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Poor devil — and yet, truly,\Nin build he is like a lord, a king.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:30.68,0:10:36.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But the gods sink far-wandering men in misery\Nwhen they spin hard sorrow even for kings."
Dialogue: 1,0:10:36.44,0:10:42.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He spoke, and coming up he gave him his right hand in\Nwelcome, and lifting his voice he spoke winged words:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:42.90,0:10:45.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Your health, father stranger! May happiness be yours
Dialogue: 1,0:10:45.94,0:10:50.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in time to come — though now\Nyou are held fast in many evils.
Dialogue: 1,0:10:50.40,0:10:53.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Father Zeus — no other god is deadlier than you:
Dialogue: 1,0:10:53.54,0:11:00.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}you have no pity on men, though you begot them yourself,\Nfor tangling them in misery and grinding pains.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:00.10,0:11:07.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I broke sweat when I saw him, and my eyes stand full\Nof tears, remembering Odysseus: for he too, I think,
Dialogue: 1,0:11:07.40,0:11:13.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}wears rags like these, and wanders somewhere among men —\Nif indeed he lives, and sees the light of the sun.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:13.98,0:11:17.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But if he is dead already, down in the house of Hades —
Dialogue: 1,0:11:17.80,0:11:21.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}ah me, blameless Odysseus! — who set me over his cattle
Dialogue: 1,0:11:21.04,0:11:24.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}when I was still a boy, in the land of the Cephallenians.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:24.90,0:11:28.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And now they are past all counting: no other man
Dialogue: 1,0:11:28.02,0:11:32.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}could have a breed of broad-browed\Ncattle bear richer, like standing wheat.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:32.38,0:11:37.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And strangers order me to drive them in for them to eat!\NThey care nothing for the son in the house,
Dialogue: 1,0:11:37.56,0:11:40.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they do not tremble at the gods'\Nwatching eye: they are wild
Dialogue: 1,0:11:40.68,0:11:43.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to divide up the goods of the master so long away.
Dialogue: 1,0:11:43.82,0:11:49.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As for me, this thought keeps rolling over and over\Nin the heart in my breast: it is a very bad thing,
Dialogue: 1,0:11:49.56,0:11:53.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}while the son lives, to go off to a country of strangers,
Dialogue: 1,0:11:53.24,0:11:56.86,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}cattle and all, to foreign men; but colder still to stay
Dialogue: 1,0:11:56.86,0:12:00.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}suffering here, sitting over cattle that others claim.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:00.20,0:12:06.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And long ago I would have run, fled to some other of\Nthe high-handed kings — for this is past all bearing —
Dialogue: 1,0:12:06.28,0:12:09.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}but still I think of that unlucky man: what if he came
Dialogue: 1,0:12:09.34,0:12:13.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}from somewhere, and made a scattering\Nof the suitors through the house!"
Dialogue: 1,0:12:13.46,0:12:16.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:12:16.24,0:12:19.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Cowherd — since you seem no bad man and no fool,
Dialogue: 1,0:12:19.48,0:12:22.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and I can see for myself that\Ngood sense has reached your heart,
Dialogue: 1,0:12:22.64,0:12:26.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I will tell you something, and swear a great oath upon it:
Dialogue: 1,0:12:26.08,0:12:30.46,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Now Zeus be my witness, first of the gods,\Nand this table of friendship,
Dialogue: 1,0:12:30.46,0:12:33.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the hearth of blameless Odysseus,\Nto which I have come:
Dialogue: 1,0:12:33.18,0:12:39.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}while you are here, Odysseus will come home;\Nand with your own eyes, if you wish it, you shall see
Dialogue: 1,0:12:39.36,0:12:42.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the killing of the suitors who play lord and master here."
Dialogue: 1,0:12:42.80,0:12:45.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then the cowherd answered him, the master of the oxen:
Dialogue: 1,0:12:45.96,0:12:49.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Ah, stranger, if only the son of\NCronos would accomplish that word!
Dialogue: 1,0:12:49.60,0:12:52.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then you would learn my strength —\Nand how my hands follow it."
Dialogue: 1,0:12:52.94,0:12:55.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And in the same way Eumaeus prayed to all the gods
Dialogue: 1,0:12:55.32,0:12:58.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that Odysseus of the many designs\Nmight come home to his own house.
Dialogue: 1,0:12:58.94,0:13:02.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So they spoke to one another of such things.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:02.18,0:13:05.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the suitors, meanwhile, were framing death and doom
Dialogue: 1,0:13:05.00,0:13:08.54,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for Telemachus — when a bird came by on their left hand:
Dialogue: 1,0:13:08.54,0:13:11.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}an eagle flying high, gripping a trembling dove.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:11.76,0:13:14.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Amphinomus rose and spoke among them:
Dialogue: 1,0:13:14.24,0:13:20.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Friends, this plan of ours will not run smooth —\Nthe killing of Telemachus. Let us think of the feast."
Dialogue: 1,0:13:20.64,0:13:23.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So spoke Amphinomus, and his word pleased them well.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:23.92,0:13:29.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}They came into the house of godlike Odysseus and\Nlaid their mantles down on the couches and chairs
Dialogue: 1,0:13:29.38,0:13:34.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and slaughtered big sheep and fat goats,\Nslaughtered sleek hogs and a heifer of the herd.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:34.18,0:13:37.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}They roasted the innards and served them round, and mixed
Dialogue: 1,0:13:37.00,0:13:41.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the wine in the mixing-bowls;\Nand the swineherd passed the cups,
Dialogue: 1,0:13:41.20,0:13:47.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and Philoetius, a leader of men, passed them bread\Nin beautiful baskets; and Melanthius poured the wine.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:47.76,0:13:51.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And their hands went out to the\Ngood things spread before them.
Dialogue: 1,0:13:51.10,0:13:54.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But Telemachus — his craft at work — seated Odysseus
Dialogue: 1,0:13:54.52,0:14:00.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}inside the strong-built hall, beside the stone threshold,\Nsetting him a battered stool and a little table;
Dialogue: 1,0:14:00.52,0:14:06.08,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}he put a portion of the innards by him,\Nand poured him wine in a golden cup, and said to him:
Dialogue: 1,0:14:06.08,0:14:08.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Sit here now among the men, and drink your wine.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:08.90,0:14:14.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}The taunts and the fists of all the suitors —\NI myself will hold them off you. This is no public house:
Dialogue: 1,0:14:14.60,0:14:17.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}it is Odysseus' house — and he won it for me.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:17.64,0:14:24.06,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And you, you suitors, hold your hearts back from insult\Nand fists, so no strife and quarreling break out."
Dialogue: 1,0:14:24.06,0:14:29.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and they all bit their lips and stared\Nin wonder at Telemachus, that he spoke so boldly.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:29.82,0:14:33.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Antinous spoke among them, Eupeithes' son:
Dialogue: 1,0:14:33.98,0:14:40.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Hard as it is, Achaeans, let us take the word of\NTelemachus — though how he threatens as he talks!
Dialogue: 1,0:14:40.14,0:14:43.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Zeus the son of Cronos would not have it; otherwise
Dialogue: 1,0:14:43.56,0:14:48.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}we would have silenced him in the\Nhalls long since — so clear a speaker."
Dialogue: 1,0:14:48.30,0:14:51.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So spoke Antinous; but he took no heed of his words.
Dialogue: 1,0:14:51.98,0:14:54.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Now the heralds were leading\Nthrough the town the holy hecatomb
Dialogue: 1,0:14:54.76,0:15:01.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}of the gods; and the long-haired Achaeans gathered\Nunder the shady grove of Apollo who shoots from afar.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:01.70,0:15:04.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And when they had roasted the\Nouter flesh and drawn it off,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:04.76,0:15:10.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}they divided the portions and feasted the glorious feast;\Nand the serving-men set beside Odysseus a share
Dialogue: 1,0:15:10.64,0:15:17.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}equal to the ones they drew themselves:\Nfor so ordered Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:17.18,0:15:23.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Athena would not let the lordly suitors hold back\Nfrom heart-stinging outrage — so that pain might sink
Dialogue: 1,0:15:23.18,0:15:26.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}still deeper into Laertes' son Odysseus.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:26.80,0:15:29.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}There was among the suitors a man versed in lawlessness:
Dialogue: 1,0:15:29.98,0:15:35.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Ctesippus was his name, and his home was on Same;\Nsure of his fabulous wealth, he had come
Dialogue: 1,0:15:35.66,0:15:39.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to woo the wife of Odysseus, the master so long away.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:39.52,0:15:42.56,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He now spoke out among the overweening suitors:
Dialogue: 1,0:15:42.56,0:15:45.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Hear me, you lordly suitors, while I say a word.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:45.52,0:15:48.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}The stranger has long had his portion — an equal one,
Dialogue: 1,0:15:48.60,0:15:52.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}as is proper: it is not fine, and it is not just, to stint
Dialogue: 1,0:15:52.66,0:15:55.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the guests of Telemachus, whoever comes to this house.
Dialogue: 1,0:15:55.98,0:15:59.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But come — let me too give him a guest-gift, so he in turn
Dialogue: 1,0:15:59.52,0:16:05.26,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}can hand a prize to the bath-woman, or to some other\Nof the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus."
Dialogue: 1,0:16:05.26,0:16:07.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking, he snatched an ox-hoof out of the basket
Dialogue: 1,0:16:07.96,0:16:11.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}where it lay, and hurled it with\Nhis thick hand. But Odysseus
Dialogue: 1,0:16:11.80,0:16:15.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}dodged it with a quick tilt of\Nhis head, and smiled in his heart
Dialogue: 1,0:16:15.10,0:16:19.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a smile most sardonic; and it struck the well-built wall.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:19.28,0:16:22.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Telemachus lashed Ctesippus with his words:
Dialogue: 1,0:16:22.52,0:16:25.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Ctesippus — count it clear profit for your own heart:
Dialogue: 1,0:16:25.60,0:16:29.04,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}you missed the stranger. He dodged the throw himself.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:29.04,0:16:32.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Otherwise I would have run you through\Nthe middle with my sharp spear,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:32.28,0:16:37.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and instead of a wedding your father would be busy\Nhere with a funeral. So: let no man show his ugliness
Dialogue: 1,0:16:37.94,0:16:41.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}in this house of mine. For now I\Ntake note and I know each thing,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:41.92,0:16:45.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the good and the worse alike. Before, I was still a child.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:45.30,0:16:47.72,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But even so we endure it and look on:
Dialogue: 1,0:16:47.72,0:16:53.52,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the flocks slaughtered, the wine drunk down,\Nthe bread — for it is hard for one man to check many.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:53.52,0:16:55.92,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Come then, do me no more evil in your malice.
Dialogue: 1,0:16:55.92,0:16:59.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But if you are set on killing me —\Nme myself — with the bronze,
Dialogue: 1,0:16:59.62,0:17:05.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}even that I would choose: it would be far better to\Ndie than look on forever at these disgraceful doings:
Dialogue: 1,0:17:05.64,0:17:09.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}guests roughly handled, and men hauling the\Nslave-women shamefully through the beautiful house."
Dialogue: 1,0:17:09.98,0:17:13.82,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and they all fell silent and still.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:13.82,0:17:17.18,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}At last Agelaus spoke, Damastor's son:
Dialogue: 1,0:17:17.18,0:17:22.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Friends — no man should take offense at a just word\Nspoken, and answer it with wrangling and hard speech.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:22.96,0:17:29.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Do not knock the stranger about, nor any other\Nof the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:29.10,0:17:31.80,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But to Telemachus, and to his mother, I would say
Dialogue: 1,0:17:31.80,0:17:35.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a gentle word — if it might find\Nfavor with the hearts of both.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:35.50,0:17:38.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}As long as the hearts in your breasts kept hope
Dialogue: 1,0:17:38.02,0:17:42.00,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}that Odysseus of the many designs\Nwould come home to his own house,
Dialogue: 1,0:17:42.00,0:17:47.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}no one could resent your waiting, or your holding\Noff the suitors in the halls: that was the better way
Dialogue: 1,0:17:47.62,0:17:51.36,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}if Odysseus had returned, come back at last to his house.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:51.36,0:17:54.10,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But now it is plain that he is past returning.
Dialogue: 1,0:17:54.10,0:17:57.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Go then, sit by your mother, and lay it out for her:
Dialogue: 1,0:17:57.14,0:18:02.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}marry the best man, the one who offers most —\Nso you may keep and enjoy all that was your father's,
Dialogue: 1,0:18:02.64,0:18:05.94,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}eating and drinking, and she may keep another's house."
Dialogue: 1,0:18:05.94,0:18:08.28,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:
Dialogue: 1,0:18:08.28,0:18:13.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"No, by Zeus, Agelaus —\Nand by the sufferings of my father,
Dialogue: 1,0:18:13.30,0:18:16.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}who far from Ithaca has perished, or still wanders:
Dialogue: 1,0:18:16.22,0:18:22.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I do not delay my mother's marriage. I urge her to wed\Nthe man she chooses; and I offer gifts past counting.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:22.96,0:18:25.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But I am ashamed to drive her\Nfrom the hall against her will
Dialogue: 1,0:18:25.60,0:18:29.96,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}with a word of compulsion.\NMay god never bring that to pass."
Dialogue: 1,0:18:29.96,0:18:33.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So spoke Telemachus. And in the suitors Pallas Athena
Dialogue: 1,0:18:33.38,0:18:37.68,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}set off unquenchable laughter,\Nand knocked their wits askew.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:37.68,0:18:40.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And now they were laughing with jaws no longer their own;
Dialogue: 1,0:18:40.84,0:18:43.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the meat they ate was dabbled in blood; and their eyes
Dialogue: 1,0:18:43.74,0:18:47.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}brimmed full of tears;\Nand the heart in them wanted to wail.
Dialogue: 1,0:18:47.76,0:18:50.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And godlike Theoclymenus spoke out among them:
Dialogue: 1,0:18:50.66,0:18:54.40,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Wretched men — what evil is this\Nyou suffer? Night — your heads
Dialogue: 1,0:18:54.40,0:18:58.44,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}are shrouded in night, and your\Nfaces, and your knees beneath;
Dialogue: 1,0:18:58.44,0:19:01.70,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}wailing has caught like fire; cheeks are streaming tears;
Dialogue: 1,0:19:01.70,0:19:05.62,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}the walls are spattered with blood,\Nand the fine crossbeams;
Dialogue: 1,0:19:05.62,0:19:09.34,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and the porch is full of ghosts —\Nthe courtyard full of them —
Dialogue: 1,0:19:09.34,0:19:12.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}ghosts hurrying down to Erebus, under the gloom; the sun
Dialogue: 1,0:19:12.58,0:19:17.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}has perished out of the sky,\Nand an evil mist runs over everything."
Dialogue: 1,0:19:17.02,0:19:20.02,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So he spoke; and they all laughed at him sweetly.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:20.02,0:19:23.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And Eurymachus, Polybus' son, began to speak among them:
Dialogue: 1,0:19:23.76,0:19:27.24,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"The stranger is raving — this new one, fresh from abroad.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:27.24,0:19:30.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Quick, young men — see him out of the house and on his way
Dialogue: 1,0:19:30.22,0:19:33.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}to the assembly-place, since he finds it night in here."
Dialogue: 1,0:19:33.74,0:19:36.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Then godlike Theoclymenus said to him:
Dialogue: 1,0:19:36.30,0:19:39.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Eurymachus, I ask no escort of you for my going.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:39.50,0:19:44.84,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}I have eyes, and ears, and both my feet,\Nand a mind in my breast in no way meanly made.
Dialogue: 1,0:19:44.84,0:19:51.14,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}With these I am going out that door — for I see evil\Ncoming on you, evil no man will escape or dodge,
Dialogue: 1,0:19:51.14,0:19:54.50,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}no man of you suitors, you who\Nin the house of godlike Odysseus
Dialogue: 1,0:19:54.50,0:19:58.12,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}batter men with outrage and devise your reckless deeds."
Dialogue: 1,0:19:58.12,0:20:03.58,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So speaking he went out of the well-settled house\Nand came to Piraeus, who received him gladly.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:03.58,0:20:09.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And the suitors all, eyeing one another, went to\Nwork needling Telemachus, laughing at his guests;
Dialogue: 1,0:20:09.30,0:20:11.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and a man of the arrogant young men would say:
Dialogue: 1,0:20:11.90,0:20:15.38,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}"Telemachus, no man is unluckier in his guests than you.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:15.38,0:20:20.98,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Look at this one you keep — this pawed-over vagrant\Nwho wants his bread and wine, who has no skill for work
Dialogue: 1,0:20:20.98,0:20:24.22,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and no strength either: a dead weight on the good earth.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:24.22,0:20:26.42,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And then the other one — he stood up to play the prophet!
Dialogue: 1,0:20:26.42,0:20:29.60,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}If you would listen to me, the profit is all yours:
Dialogue: 1,0:20:29.60,0:20:31.90,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}let us pitch your guests into a many-benched ship
Dialogue: 1,0:20:31.90,0:20:36.32,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}and send them to the Sicilians.\NThere they would fetch you a worthy price."
Dialogue: 1,0:20:36.32,0:20:39.74,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}So the suitors talked; but he took no heed of their words.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:39.74,0:20:42.48,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}He watched his father in silence — waiting, always,
Dialogue: 1,0:20:42.48,0:20:45.66,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}for the moment he would lay his\Nhands on the shameless suitors.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:45.66,0:20:49.16,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}And she — she had set her\Nbeautiful chair across from them:
Dialogue: 1,0:20:49.16,0:20:54.64,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}Icarius' daughter, watchful-minded Penelope,\Nand heard each man's word in the halls.
Dialogue: 1,0:20:54.64,0:20:56.76,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}For laughing they had made their dinner ready —
Dialogue: 1,0:20:56.76,0:21:01.20,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}a sweet meal and a hearty one,\Nfor they had slaughtered lavishly.
Dialogue: 1,0:21:01.20,0:21:06.30,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}But no supper could ever be more graceless than the\Nsupper a goddess and a strong man were soon now to set
Dialogue: 1,0:21:06.30,0:21:11.23,Narration,,0,0,0,,{\fad(60,120)}before them. For they had begun it:\Nthe shameful devising was theirs.
